.TITLE "find"

.DIV "DESCRIPTION"
Searches for files and directories on macOS based on various criteria. This command
traverses a directory hierarchy to locate files matching expressions such as
name, type, size, modification time, and permissions. It is a powerful but
complex utility for __filesystem navigation__ and management.

.DIV "ALIAS"
**locate**

.DIV "TLDR"
**find __/path/to/search__ -name "__*.txt__"**
Finds all files ending with "__.txt__" within the specified __/path/to/search__ directory.
> __/path/to/search__: The starting directory for the search. | __"*.txt"__: The filename pattern to match.

**find . -type d -name "__build__"**
Finds all directories named "__build__" within the current directory and its subdirectories.
> __.**: The current directory. | __-type d__: Specifies to search for directories. | __"build"__: The directory name to match.

**find __/path/to/search__ -mtime +7**
Finds files modified more than 7 days ago within the specified __/path/to/search__.
> __/path/to/search__: The starting directory for the search. | __-mtime +7__: Files modified more than 7 * 24 hours ago.

**find __/path/to/search__ -size +1G**
Finds files larger than 1 Gigabyte within the specified __/path/to/search__.
> __/path/to/search__: The starting directory for the search. | __-size +1G__: Files larger than 1 Gigabyte.

**find . -name "__*.log__" -delete**
Finds all files ending with "__.log__" in the current directory and deletes them.
> __.**: The current directory. | __"*.log"__: The filename pattern. | __-delete__: Deletes the found files.

.DIV "SUMMARY"
`find` is incredibly flexible but can be slow on large filesystems if not used
efficiently. Always specify the starting directory clearly. Be very careful
when using actions like `-delete` or `-exec` as they can lead to irreversible
data loss. Consider using `mdfind` for faster metadata-based searches.

.DIV "SEE ALSO"
**mac:mdfind** **mac:grep**

